Adversarial acceptance found a real P1 recruiter-quality defect in the merged runtime: public hiring-post discovery persisted person recruiters whose parsed title was a LinkedIn/search artifact (for example `LinkedIn https://de`) without a validated profile URL.

This coherent persistence-boundary fix:
- blocks URL/search-result artifacts from proactive-public-web person contacts;
- preserves the explicit employer recruiting-contact path;
- removes malformed records already produced by the real runtime;
- keeps all outreach/application safety gates unchanged.

The fix is intentionally at the database persistence boundary so every current producer is protected, including the direct public-hiring-post persistence path.

Security follow-up to #309 and #291.

PR #309 intended refresh rotation to serialize against Identity security changes by locking the user's `AspNetUsers` row and revalidating `SecurityStamp` after the lock. Its merged implementation instead used a PostgreSQL advisory lock that ordinary Identity writes do not participate in, and it did not revalidate the stamp after acquiring that lock.

This patch:
- replaces the advisory lock with a transaction-scoped `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` on the exact Identity user row;
- rereads and revalidates the current security stamp after that row lock is held;
- keeps the existing exact-state refresh-family compare-and-swap underneath the row lock;
- removes the now-unused advisory-lock key derivation.

This closes the race where password/role/2FA/provider security changes could occur between the first stamp validation and refresh-family rotation.

No schema migration, route change, token-format change, provider action, production deployment, or weakening of existing replay protection.

Merge only after the exact final head passes the complete required CI/security/recovery gates.
